dyanega@pop.ucr.edu (Doug Yanega) writes:

> I don't like leaving "stashes", basically - if it's a useful item, I
> don't like to leave it behind.

Neither do I. But I still don't have any serious weight management
problems. I occasionally allow myself to become burdened for a short
while when not in an overly dangerous area, but otherwise I make the
point of staying unburdened.

The key is to not carry unessential items with you. The only items
that I stash at all are potions and useless scrolls before I have a
bag of holding and spellbooks for wizards. Everything else is either
left in place or carried.

>  So, if I've got, say, 40K in gold,

Proceed directly to the nearest priest and donate it. After maxing
your AC don't pick any more. It is possible to carry up to 60K gold in
a blessed bag of holding and still be unburdened if it happens
relatively early in the game when you don't have too many potions in
BoH, yet. 

> 5 amulets,

No problem at all. Their weight is practically negligible compared to
other stuff. 

> 5 artifact weapons,

These are mostly useless unless you wan't an artifact collection.
Stick with one or at most two best and leave others behind. Though, I
routinely ascend with 5-6 artifacts in BoH while unburdened so again,
they are not a problem. 

> some spare boots and helmets and cloaks,

These area mostly useless. The only spare boots that I bother to carry
are water walking and levitation when I don't have ring of levitation. 

> 20 scrolls,

No problem. You can have dozens of them. 

> 10 rings,

Absolutely no problem. 

> the high-payoff food (lembas, candy, jelly, pancakes),

Depending on what other things you carry, these may have too bad
usefullness/weight ratio. When I have to lighten up my load, extra
foodstuff is the first thing to go. A couple of emergency rations are
all that you *really* need, anything over that is luxury.

> 5 crucial spellbooks,

Not too useful unless you are a wizard. And even then you are better
off leaving them in some level that you can instantly teleport to with
the Eye of Misspelling. 

Of course, I play rather fast games but generally I don't need more
than one reread of any spellbook. Leaving them beside down stairs of
their levels is just about the all (non-wizard) stashing that I do for
them. 

> 20 wands (mostly digging, teleport, and sleep),

No problem. 

> and an assortment of tools including a pickaxe and a blessed tinning
> kit,

Problem. Big problem.

If you don't like stashing, pickaxes are a big no-no. The only place
where you really need one is the wine cellar mine's end and usually
some dwarf has it on that level or the one before so you don't have to
carry it with you there.

In all other places you can use wands. Wands of digging are plentiful
and it is very rare for them to run out if you don't go crazy with
them. (Always remember to leave enough charges to get into wizard's
tower at the end). 

The same thing holds for tinning kits, crystal balls, and cans of
grease. They are too heavy for non-stashers. The only exceptions are
the two artifact crystall balls, but with them you have to ditch most
of your food and perhaps even potions to stay unburdened. I
occasionally take a normal crystal ball to the planes if one is
generated in the early dungeon and I'm running short on potions but
that is quite rare. 

> that makes my BoH pretty darn heavy right there.

You forgot to mention the ~50 potions that you will be carrying in
the end (at least 20 holy water, 10 different healing potions waiting
for alchemy, and 20 potions waiting to be turned into water). 

